.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESS-EXISTS-SECRET 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ess-exists-secret \- whether a secret exists, as an exit code for a script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ess exists-secret
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers whether a secret exists. Written to be branched on rather than
read: a shell treats a successful exit as true, so the answer is the exit
status.
.PP
.B "true"
or
.B "false"
also goes to standard output, and nothing else does \(em no JSON, no
metadata, nothing to cut out of the way with
.BR sed (1).
So the same command serves
.B if
and
.BR "$(\|.\|.\|.\|)" .
.PP
The question has three answers, not two. Alongside "yes" and "no" there
is "could not tell": an expired session, an unreachable gateway, a
refused permission. Those exit
.BR 2 ,
so a script never reads an outage as an absent secret and goes on to
create one that was already there, or skip a step that mattered. A script
that only branches on
.B if
still gets the safe outcome, because
.B 2
is not success either and the true branch is not taken on an answer
nobody actually got.
.PP
Asks
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1)
rather than
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
and that is deliberate. get-secret answers with the decrypted value, so
asking it whether a secret exists would mean holding
.IR ess:get\-secret \(em
permission to read the password rather than to know the name is taken
\(em decrypting it, sending the plaintext across the wire, and leaving an
audit entry indistinguishable from somebody actually reading it. None of
that is any part of the question.
.PP
So this needs
.I ess:list\-secrets
instead, and never touches the value. Note that an application principal
granted only
.I ess:get\-secret
for its own secrets therefore cannot use this command; the
.I reader
role and the administrators hold both.
.PP
Needs
.IR ess:list-secrets ,
because that is the call it makes. A caller without it gets
.B 2
rather than a wrong answer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the secret to look for. Required. A missing
.B \-\-name
is a broken command line rather than an absent secret, and exits
.BR 2 .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The secret exists.
.B "true"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
The secret does not exist.
.B "false"
is written to standard output.
.TP
.B 2
The question could not be answered: no
.BR \-\-name ,
an unreachable or unauthenticated gateway, a refused permission, or any
other error. The reason is written to standard error and standard output
is left empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
Act only if it is not already there:
.RS
.nf
if ! euclid\-cli ess exists\-secret \-n db-password > /dev/null; then
    echo "creating db-password"
fi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tell the three answers apart, which any script that acts on absence
should do:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli ess exists\-secret \-n db-password > /dev/null
case $? in
    0) echo "already there" ;;
    1) echo "absent" ;;
    2) echo "could not tell \(em leaving it alone" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ess (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-list-secrets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-create-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ess-get-secret (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-exists-queue (1)
